WEBVTT

00:00:00.043 --> 00:00:01.044 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Builder.

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.784 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Blueprint.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.074 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson one.

00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:05.355 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Introduction.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.616 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Education only.

00:00:08.175 --> 00:00:09.489 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Not financial advice.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.061 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is general information and

00:00:12.061 --> 00:00:14.264 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
doesn't consider your objectives,

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.164 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
financial situation or needs.

00:00:16.684 --> 00:00:18.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto, a digital coin, is high risk;

00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:21.319 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you could lose all the money you put in.

00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:23.890 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past performance is not a reliable

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.588 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
indicator of future results.

00:00:26.638 --> 00:00:29.031 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The prices are public historical prices

00:00:29.031 --> 00:00:31.209 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at the end of each day, marked Example.

00:00:32.342 --> 00:00:34.241 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No order is sent from this lesson.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.139 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end of this series you will

00:00:37.139 --> 00:00:38.927 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
understand what a grid bot does.

00:00:39.324 --> 00:00:40.776 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is a small program that

00:00:40.776 --> 00:00:42.341 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
places these orders for you.

00:00:42.797 --> 00:00:44.696 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will learn when it can capture

00:00:44.696 --> 00:00:46.260 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces, and where it loses.

00:00:46.508 --> 00:00:48.519 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will know how to plan one, test

00:00:48.519 --> 00:00:50.418 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
it on paper, run it, check it, and

00:00:50.418 --> 00:00:52.466 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
change the capital you give to bots

00:00:52.466 --> 00:00:54.327 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
only under written risk rules.

00:00:54.847 --> 00:00:56.295 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is education, not

00:00:56.295 --> 00:00:57.662 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a promise of results.

00:00:58.182 --> 00:00:59.792 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price often moves sideways,

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:01.543 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bouncing between the same top

00:01:01.543 --> 00:01:03.293 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and bottom instead of leaving.

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:05.523 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid is a ladder of waiting

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:07.232 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
orders, called limit orders,

00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:08.719 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
built for that bouncing.

00:01:09.239 --> 00:01:11.083 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When price runs one way and stays

00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:13.262 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
there, the grid has nothing left to do.

00:01:13.642 --> 00:01:15.395 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
And if price falls through the

00:01:15.395 --> 00:01:17.078 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, coin means the crypto

00:01:17.078 --> 00:01:18.901 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
itself, and you are left holding

00:01:18.901 --> 00:01:20.633 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the coin, and it is worth less.

00:01:21.564 --> 00:01:22.904 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end you will know

00:01:22.904 --> 00:01:24.189 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
what a grid bot places.

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:27.397 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will see that idea on

00:01:27.397 --> 00:01:29.372 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
one real path from 2023.

00:01:30.572 --> 00:01:32.081 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will be able to say the

00:01:32.081 --> 00:01:33.533 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stop rule in one sentence.

00:01:34.487 --> 00:01:36.333 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When a chart says Example, it

00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:38.179 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
means a real past price used

00:01:38.179 --> 00:01:40.025 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
as an example, not a forecast.

00:01:41.075 --> 00:01:42.317 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto is a digital

00:01:42.317 --> 00:01:43.714 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
asset, such as bitcoin.

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:45.910 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You do not need to have bought

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:47.195 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
any before this lesson.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:49.541 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An exchange is the website or app

00:01:49.541 --> 00:01:51.873 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
where you swap Australian dollars for

00:01:51.873 --> 00:01:53.913 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crypto, and crypto back to dollars.

00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:56.109 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course does not name one,

00:01:56.109 --> 00:01:57.785 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it does not send an order.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.094 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Cash is money you can spend.

00:02:00.496 --> 00:02:02.332 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here that means dollars, or a

00:02:02.332 --> 00:02:04.407 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stablecoin, a crypto coin built

00:02:04.407 --> 00:02:05.923 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
to stay at one US dollar.

00:02:06.443 --> 00:02:08.510 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We treat it like cash in this lesson,

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.242 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
but it can drop below a dollar.

00:02:10.671 --> 00:02:12.862 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Coin is the crypto, such as bitcoin.

00:02:13.912 --> 00:02:16.259 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order is an instruction to buy or sell.

00:02:16.711 --> 00:02:18.518 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy order swaps cash for coin.

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.941 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell order swaps coin back to

00:02:20.941 --> 00:02:23.072 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cash, and you can only sell coin you

00:02:23.072 --> 00:02:24.975 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
already hold, on a spot account.

00:02:25.495 --> 00:02:27.132 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A spot account is the normal

00:02:27.132 --> 00:02:28.484 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
account on an exchange.

00:02:29.004 --> 00:02:30.400 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You own the coin you buy,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.741 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and nothing is borrowed.

00:02:32.623 --> 00:02:34.393 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot is software that places

00:02:34.393 --> 00:02:36.015 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and refills orders for you.

00:02:36.535 --> 00:02:38.378 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You start with some cash and some

00:02:38.378 --> 00:02:40.054 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, so there is something to

00:02:40.054 --> 00:02:41.786 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buy with and something to sell.

00:02:41.835 --> 00:02:44.187 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buy orders

00:02:44.187 --> 00:02:46.539 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on lines below the current price and

00:02:46.539 --> 00:02:48.892 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell orders on lines above it, inside

00:02:48.892 --> 00:02:50.695 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a top and a bottom you choose.

00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:52.891 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The space between your top and

00:02:52.891 --> 00:02:54.679 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your bottom is called the range.

00:02:54.772 --> 00:02:56.878 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sells need some coin, so a grid

00:02:56.878 --> 00:02:58.827 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
starts with part cash and part

00:02:58.827 --> 00:03:00.777 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, and that is a description,

00:03:00.777 --> 00:03:02.648 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not advice about what to hold.

00:03:03.698 --> 00:03:05.404 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A limit order is an order that

00:03:05.404 --> 00:03:06.968 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
waits at a price you choose.

00:03:07.488 --> 00:03:09.052 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not chase the price.

00:03:09.472 --> 00:03:11.036 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Chasing the price would mean

00:03:11.036 --> 00:03:12.488 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
moving the order to follow

00:03:12.488 --> 00:03:14.053 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wherever the price has gone.

00:03:14.902 --> 00:03:16.977 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order fills when the price reaches

00:03:16.977 --> 00:03:18.709 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
its line and the trade happens.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.377 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Until then, the order is only waiting.

00:03:22.427 --> 00:03:24.382 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a ladder leaned on a wall.

00:03:24.524 --> 00:03:26.870 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The rungs wait, and they do not chase you.

00:03:27.146 --> 00:03:29.436 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A rung is one price where an order waits.

00:03:29.843 --> 00:03:32.022 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The gap is the space between two rungs.

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:34.356 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The ladder is the analogy, and the

00:03:34.356 --> 00:03:36.627 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
chart beside it is the real past path.

00:03:37.677 --> 00:03:39.088 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top rung

00:03:39.088 --> 00:03:40.217 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the bottom rung.

00:03:40.737 --> 00:03:42.860 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the rungs between them.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.500 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is the written rule for leaving.

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:48.193 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top line and the

00:03:48.193 --> 00:03:50.036 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom line, taken from the path.

00:03:50.515 --> 00:03:52.003 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Hindsight: this range is

00:03:52.003 --> 00:03:53.268 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
drawn after the fact.

00:03:53.788 --> 00:03:56.988 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The chart is bitcoin's real daily

00:03:56.988 --> 00:04:00.532 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closes from 19 July to 16 October 2023.

00:04:01.052 --> 00:04:02.504 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Every close in this window

00:04:02.504 --> 00:04:04.236 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stayed between those two lines.

00:04:05.049 --> 00:04:07.455 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Six gaps of about 900 dollars each sit

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:10.016 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
between the bottom line and the top line.

00:04:10.536 --> 00:04:12.763 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each gap is about 900 dollars:

00:04:12.763 --> 00:04:14.990 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the top line minus the bottom

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:16.475 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
line, divided by six.

00:04:16.995 --> 00:04:18.340 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is one step between

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:19.909 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines, not the whole range.

00:04:20.429 --> 00:04:22.267 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Seven lines make those six gaps.

00:04:22.787 --> 00:04:24.575 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the prices on the

00:04:24.575 --> 00:04:26.251 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
rungs where limit orders wait.

00:04:27.238 --> 00:04:28.596 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Buy orders wait on lines

00:04:28.596 --> 00:04:29.954 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
below the current price.

00:04:30.474 --> 00:04:32.429 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Sell orders wait on lines above it.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.410 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fill changes what you hold: a buy

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:38.075 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fill adds coin, and a sell fill adds cash.

00:04:38.595 --> 00:04:39.769 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You choose the range,

00:04:39.769 --> 00:04:41.054 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the gaps and the money.

00:04:41.363 --> 00:04:42.815 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot places each order.

00:04:43.822 --> 00:04:45.672 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
After a fill, the bot places the

00:04:45.672 --> 00:04:47.744 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
opposite order one rung up or down.

00:04:48.264 --> 00:04:50.443 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy fill gets a sell one rung higher.

00:04:50.829 --> 00:04:53.059 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell fill gets a buy one rung lower.

00:04:53.579 --> 00:04:55.199 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You still write the stop, and

00:04:55.199 --> 00:04:56.875 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you act when that rule is met.

00:04:57.106 --> 00:04:59.341 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop for you

00:04:59.341 --> 00:05:01.464 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on, and

00:05:01.464 --> 00:05:03.531 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you write which one before you start.

00:05:04.265 --> 00:05:05.606 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a market stall.

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:08.152 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stallholder buys stock a little

00:05:08.152 --> 00:05:10.266 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cheaper and sells it a little dearer,

00:05:10.266 --> 00:05:11.886 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and pays a fee on every sale.

00:05:12.337 --> 00:05:13.906 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Exchanges charge a small

00:05:13.906 --> 00:05:14.952 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fee on each trade.

00:05:15.472 --> 00:05:17.216 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid can do that while price

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:18.741 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces inside the range.

00:05:19.261 --> 00:05:21.782 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bounce means price moves down and back

00:05:21.782 --> 00:05:24.151 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
up, or up and back down, and stays inside.

00:05:24.671 --> 00:05:26.908 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One buy at a lower line and the later

00:05:26.908 --> 00:05:28.990 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell of that same coin is one pair.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:31.690 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On these daily closes, the July to

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.951 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
October example window finished

00:05:33.951 --> 00:05:36.050 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
only three pairs, each a buy and a

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.149 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell on lines next to each other.

00:05:38.669 --> 00:05:40.401 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sell line is higher, so the

00:05:40.401 --> 00:05:42.244 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cash that comes back is more than

00:05:42.244 --> 00:05:44.200 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the cash that left, before the fee.

00:05:44.493 --> 00:05:45.945 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A fee is what the exchange

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:47.510 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
charges when an order fills.

00:05:47.691 --> 00:05:49.981 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of what the pair makes.

00:05:50.043 --> 00:05:51.551 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the fees are larger than

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:53.171 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that, the pair keeps nothing.

00:05:53.344 --> 00:05:54.588 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is not a promise.

00:05:55.108 --> 00:05:56.912 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair finishes only if price

00:05:56.912 --> 00:05:58.785 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
does bounce and both orders fill.

00:05:59.835 --> 00:06:01.085 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The daily close is the

00:06:01.085 --> 00:06:02.314 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
last price of the day.

00:06:02.785 --> 00:06:04.585 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto trades all day, so the

00:06:04.585 --> 00:06:05.993 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
day is cut at a set time.

00:06:06.513 --> 00:06:08.835 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This lesson uses the end of each

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:10.978 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
UTC day, the world's standard

00:06:10.978 --> 00:06:13.479 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
clock, which is 11am Sydney time in

00:06:13.479 --> 00:06:15.890 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daylight saving, 10am otherwise.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.410 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a simple candle that shows

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.086 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the day's high, low and close.

00:06:20.573 --> 00:06:22.503 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Traders call it a candle because

00:06:22.503 --> 00:06:24.432 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the full version has a thick body

00:06:24.432 --> 00:06:26.164 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and a thin wick, like a candle.

00:06:26.596 --> 00:06:28.104 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we only need the high,

00:06:28.104 --> 00:06:29.333 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the low and the close.

00:06:29.727 --> 00:06:32.907 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This candle is 19 July 2023.

00:06:33.427 --> 00:06:34.656 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high, the low, and

00:06:34.656 --> 00:06:35.941 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the close are labelled.

00:06:36.935 --> 00:06:39.064 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The example is Bitcoin's public

00:06:39.064 --> 00:06:40.720 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daily price in US dollars.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.417 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
From 19 July 2023 to 16 October

00:06:44.417 --> 00:06:47.341 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023, every one of those days

00:06:47.341 --> 00:06:49.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closed inside the range.

00:06:50.403 --> 00:06:52.088 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The close crossed the seven

00:06:52.088 --> 00:06:53.847 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines 28 times in that window.

00:06:54.367 --> 00:06:56.678 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The count is an example, not a forecast.

00:06:57.198 --> 00:06:58.986 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We do not need to read each day.

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:00.595 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The shape is the lesson,

00:07:00.595 --> 00:07:02.008 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it is marked Example.

00:07:02.528 --> 00:07:05.284 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Worked check: 19 July

00:07:05.284 --> 00:07:08.365 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023 to 16 October 2023.

00:07:08.885 --> 00:07:10.393 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The low is the bottom line.

00:07:10.873 --> 00:07:12.339 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high is the top line.

00:07:12.859 --> 00:07:14.703 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Nothing on this card is an order.

00:07:15.666 --> 00:07:18.537 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Those 28 crossings, each time the price

00:07:18.537 --> 00:07:20.958 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crosses a line, are not 28 results.

00:07:21.478 --> 00:07:23.656 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A crossing only means the close moved

00:07:23.656 --> 00:07:25.723 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
from one side of a line to the other.

00:07:26.143 --> 00:07:27.854 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A pair is finished only when a

00:07:27.854 --> 00:07:29.708 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buy and its later sell have both

00:07:29.708 --> 00:07:31.440 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled, and only after the fee.

00:07:31.939 --> 00:07:33.839 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The price can cross many lines and

00:07:33.839 --> 00:07:35.738 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
still leave you holding coin if it

00:07:35.738 --> 00:07:37.358 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
walks out through the bottom.

00:07:37.895 --> 00:07:39.403 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You can buy part of a coin.

00:07:39.952 --> 00:07:42.911 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we buy one hundredth

00:07:42.953 --> 00:07:44.014 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
of a bitcoin, 0.01.

00:07:44.014 --> 00:07:47.277 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
900 dollars times 0.01 bitcoin

00:07:47.277 --> 00:07:49.800 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is 9 dollars before fees.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:53.301 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In this example a buy waiting at the

00:07:53.301 --> 00:07:56.591 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower of these two lines was reached on

00:07:56.591 --> 00:07:59.572 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
17 August 2023, and the sell one line

00:07:59.572 --> 00:08:02.553 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above was reached on 29 August 2023.

00:08:03.073 --> 00:08:04.860 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The card shows the gap, then the

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:06.425 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
two fees, then what is left.

00:08:06.734 --> 00:08:09.940 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fee is 0.1 percent of the whole

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.815 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
trade, not of the profit: about 27

00:08:12.815 --> 00:08:15.579 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cents on the 267 dollar buy and

00:08:15.579 --> 00:08:18.454 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
about 28 cents on the 276 dollar

00:08:18.454 --> 00:08:21.107 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell, about 54 cents together.

00:08:21.627 --> 00:08:23.452 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is about 8 dollars 46

00:08:23.452 --> 00:08:25.537 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
after fees, for one small pair.

00:08:26.057 --> 00:08:27.956 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It happens only if the price comes

00:08:27.956 --> 00:08:29.632 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
back up through the sell line.

00:08:29.736 --> 00:08:31.468 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the price leaves through the

00:08:31.468 --> 00:08:33.256 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, you still hold the coin,

00:08:33.256 --> 00:08:34.820 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the pair never finishes.

00:08:35.012 --> 00:08:36.800 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This card is not money you made.

00:08:37.198 --> 00:08:39.553 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past results don't predict future results.

00:08:46.603 --> 00:08:48.391 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Now watch what you hold when the

00:08:48.391 --> 00:08:50.458 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
price leaves the range and stays out.

00:08:50.837 --> 00:08:52.513 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Above the range the sells have

00:08:52.513 --> 00:08:54.301 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding cash,

00:08:54.301 --> 00:08:55.754 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with nothing left to sell.

00:08:56.075 --> 00:08:57.696 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Below the range the buys have

00:08:57.696 --> 00:08:59.148 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding

00:08:59.148 --> 00:09:00.880 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, with nothing left to buy.

00:09:01.257 --> 00:09:02.649 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is why the way down

00:09:02.649 --> 00:09:04.408 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
matters most: every buy fills,

00:09:04.408 --> 00:09:06.166 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you are left holding coin.

00:09:08.216 --> 00:09:10.451 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here is that stop rule on the real path.

00:09:10.872 --> 00:09:13.257 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It triggers if a daily close is outside

00:09:13.257 --> 00:09:15.643 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the range, through the top or the bottom,

00:09:15.643 --> 00:09:18.178 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the next daily close is still outside.

00:09:18.698 --> 00:09:20.150 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then the idea is finished.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:22.624 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot, which cancels the

00:09:22.624 --> 00:09:24.494 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
waiting orders, and do what you

00:09:24.494 --> 00:09:26.587 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wrote: keep the coin or swap to cash.

00:09:27.107 --> 00:09:29.089 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot's automatic stop is a price

00:09:29.089 --> 00:09:30.777 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that can fire during the day.

00:09:31.297 --> 00:09:32.996 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course uses the two daily

00:09:32.996 --> 00:09:34.832 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closes, unless your written plan

00:09:34.832 --> 00:09:36.620 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
says you turned on a price stop.

00:09:37.052 --> 00:09:38.392 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a teaching rule,

00:09:38.392 --> 00:09:39.570 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not a recommendation.

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:41.710 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is your written rule

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:43.163 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
for when you stop the bot.

00:09:43.396 --> 00:09:45.553 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On 23 October 2023 the close

00:09:45.553 --> 00:09:47.993 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
finished above the top line, and

00:09:47.993 --> 00:09:50.338 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day was still above it.

00:09:55.388 --> 00:09:56.840 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the top, the sells

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:58.516 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
have filled and you hold cash.

00:09:58.625 --> 00:10:00.469 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the bottom, the buys have

00:10:00.469 --> 00:10:02.312 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you hold coin that you

00:10:02.312 --> 00:10:04.324 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bought above where the price is now.

00:10:04.683 --> 00:10:06.135 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That loss can keep growing

00:10:06.135 --> 00:10:07.252 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
until the stop acts.

00:10:07.558 --> 00:10:09.401 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot either way, then

00:10:09.401 --> 00:10:10.965 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you follow the written rule.

00:10:11.074 --> 00:10:12.862 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop

00:10:12.862 --> 00:10:14.761 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on.

00:10:15.034 --> 00:10:16.456 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
EXAMPLE, made up: three

00:10:16.456 --> 00:10:18.035 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buys fill on the way down.

00:10:18.555 --> 00:10:21.855 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth of a bitcoin

00:10:21.855 --> 00:10:25.154 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at 27,630 is 276 dollars 30.

00:10:26.838 --> 00:10:32.372 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 26,730 is 267 dollars 30.

00:10:34.054 --> 00:10:39.236 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 25,830 is 258 dollars 30.

00:10:40.586 --> 00:10:43.226 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Together that is 801 dollars 90.

00:10:43.989 --> 00:10:48.196 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Three buys of 0.01 is 0.03 bitcoin.

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:52.006 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
At 24,000, that is 720 dollars.

00:10:52.526 --> 00:10:54.793 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A little below the range, at a

00:10:54.793 --> 00:10:57.158 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
made-up price of 24,000, that

00:10:57.158 --> 00:10:59.228 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin is worth 720 dollars.

00:10:59.748 --> 00:11:01.374 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You are down 81 dollars 90

00:11:01.374 --> 00:11:03.309 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
before fees, and the loss keeps

00:11:03.309 --> 00:11:05.168 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
growing until your stop acts.

00:11:06.218 --> 00:11:07.793 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A common mistake is thinking

00:11:07.793 --> 00:11:09.301 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bot predicts the price.

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:10.840 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not.

00:11:11.406 --> 00:11:12.188 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It only waits.

00:11:12.558 --> 00:11:14.066 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Another mistake is starting

00:11:14.066 --> 00:11:15.240 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with no written stop.

00:11:15.654 --> 00:11:16.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
There is no promise of

00:11:16.883 --> 00:11:18.168 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
results in this lesson.

00:11:18.407 --> 00:11:20.014 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Numbers marked Example are

00:11:20.014 --> 00:11:21.692 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
teaching numbers, not advice.

00:11:22.742 --> 00:11:25.006 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper or demo trading is like driving

00:11:25.006 --> 00:11:26.978 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with learner plates, or training

00:11:26.978 --> 00:11:29.022 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wheels: you practise with pretend

00:11:29.022 --> 00:11:30.848 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
money before anything is real.

00:11:31.368 --> 00:11:32.885 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson 7 shows you how.

00:11:33.941 --> 00:11:35.069 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps one to six.

00:11:35.769 --> 00:11:37.457 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This introduction is step one.

00:11:38.157 --> 00:11:39.218 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then what you need.

00:11:39.848 --> 00:11:41.375 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then exactly what a grid

00:11:41.375 --> 00:11:42.826 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot does, order by order.

00:11:43.826 --> 00:11:44.943 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then market regimes.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:47.587 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then choosing grid spacing and range.

00:11:48.226 --> 00:11:49.832 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then entry and exit rules.

00:11:50.548 --> 00:11:51.848 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps seven to eleven.

00:11:52.548 --> 00:11:53.632 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then a paper test.

00:11:54.352 --> 00:11:55.414 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then weekly checks.

00:11:56.151 --> 00:11:57.340 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then common mistakes.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.051 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then scaling up under written rules.

00:12:00.511 --> 00:12:01.902 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then an opt-in lesson on

00:12:01.902 --> 00:12:03.988 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
liquidation and your complete plan.

00:12:05.038 --> 00:12:06.937 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The next lesson is a checklist you

00:12:06.937 --> 00:12:08.837 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
write on paper, before any screen.

00:12:09.658 --> 00:12:11.989 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In short: a grid waits, it buys lower and

00:12:11.989 --> 00:12:14.620 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sells higher while price bounces, the fees

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:16.950 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
come out of what the pair makes, and if a

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:19.506 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daily close leaves and stays out, you stop

00:12:19.506 --> 00:12:21.761 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bot and follow the rule you wrote.

00:12:22.845 --> 00:12:23.711 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Check yourself.

00:12:24.231 --> 00:12:25.683 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Why does a grid start with

00:12:25.683 --> 00:12:27.136 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
some coin as well as cash?

00:12:27.299 --> 00:12:28.584 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Because the sell orders

00:12:28.584 --> 00:12:30.093 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
need coin you already hold.

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:33.958 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example pair, why is the net about

00:12:33.958 --> 00:12:36.716 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
8 dollars 46 and not the full 9 dollars?

00:12:37.236 --> 00:12:38.801 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The two fees come out first.

00:12:39.963 --> 00:12:42.030 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price closes above your top line, and

00:12:42.030 --> 00:12:44.209 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day's close is still above it.

00:12:44.566 --> 00:12:46.354 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
What do you do, and who does it?

00:12:46.675 --> 00:12:48.462 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot and follow your

00:12:48.462 --> 00:12:50.306 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
written rule, by hand, unless you

00:12:50.306 --> 00:12:52.150 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
turned on the bot's stop setting.

00:12:53.206 --> 00:12:53.956 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Your turn.

00:12:54.403 --> 00:12:56.135 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On paper, write the top and the

00:12:56.135 --> 00:12:58.090 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, one gap, and the stop rule.

00:12:59.193 --> 00:13:00.925 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Say what you will do if a daily

00:13:00.925 --> 00:13:02.601 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
close leaves the range and the

00:13:02.601 --> 00:13:04.170 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
next close is still outside.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:06.086 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Pause the video and write

00:13:06.086 --> 00:13:07.706 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your answer before you go on.

00:13:08.739 --> 00:13:10.806 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No real order is sent in this lesson.

00:13:11.294 --> 00:13:13.196 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper practice comes in Lesson

00:13:13.196 --> 00:13:14.807 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
7, and real money only after

00:13:14.807 --> 00:13:16.636 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your written graduation rule.

00:13:17.156 --> 00:13:19.163 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A graduation rule is the written

00:13:19.163 --> 00:13:20.873 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
test you pass on paper first.

00:13:21.405 --> 00:13:22.415 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You draw the idea.

00:13:22.935 --> 00:13:24.728 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a screen appears later, it

00:13:24.728 --> 00:13:26.444 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
should be a copy of the page,

00:13:26.444 --> 00:13:28.003 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and only on paper or demo.

00:13:29.203 --> 00:13:31.564 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring a notebook, and no secrets: no

00:13:31.564 --> 00:13:34.178 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
seed phrase, private key or API secret.

00:13:34.698 --> 00:13:36.807 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A seed phrase or private key is the

00:13:36.807 --> 00:13:38.992 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
master password to a crypto wallet.

00:13:39.512 --> 00:13:41.467 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An API secret is a key that lets an

00:13:41.467 --> 00:13:43.410 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
app place trades on your account,

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.431 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and sometimes move money out of it.

00:13:46.481 --> 00:13:48.173 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair, in the next lesson,

00:13:48.173 --> 00:13:49.789 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is the two things you swap,

00:13:49.789 --> 00:13:51.558 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
like bitcoin and US dollars.

00:13:52.608 --> 00:13:54.849 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buys below

00:13:54.849 --> 00:13:57.089 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the current price and waiting sells

00:13:57.089 --> 00:13:59.181 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above it, inside a range you choose.

00:14:00.381 --> 00:14:01.498 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A finished pair buys

00:14:01.498 --> 00:14:02.783 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower and sells higher.

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.280 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of what the pair makes.

00:14:05.342 --> 00:14:07.400 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example that was about 8

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.622 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
dollars 46, and it is not a promise.

00:14:10.822 --> 00:14:12.219 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the top and

00:14:12.219 --> 00:14:13.392 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you hold mostly cash.

00:14:13.841 --> 00:14:15.182 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the bottom

00:14:15.182 --> 00:14:16.579 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you hold mostly coin.

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:19.973 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a daily close leaves the range and the

00:14:19.973 --> 00:14:22.327 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
next close is still outside, you stop the

00:14:22.327 --> 00:14:24.253 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot and follow the rule you wrote.

00:14:25.453 --> 00:14:27.687 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Next lesson: What you are going to need.

00:14:28.076 --> 00:14:29.696 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring your notes: your range,

00:14:29.696 --> 00:14:31.316 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
